//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// Asking for a scratchpad on a machine that has none is a compile error (#341).
//
// The fleet's `SMEM` is per-SM shared memory: storage a program allocates
// because it put something there. x86 has nothing of the kind. The nearest
// thing is the per-core L2, which is a cache -- a tile is resident there only
// while the hardware has no better use for the lines.
//
// So fleet/xeon-e5-2666v3.vx declares no `SMEM`, and this is what that buys.
// The alternative was to declare the per-core L2 under that name to keep the
// shared vocabulary complete, which would have *admitted* this program on a
// capacity number that does not mean what it means on any other SKU. The
// program would have been told it had a scratchpad and been given a hope.
//
// A machine file is a description of hardware before it is a row in a table. If
// the hardware does not have it, the file does not declare it, and the compiler
// says so before anything is rented.
//
// The same program compiles against fleet/a100-80.vx, which does have one --
// that half is checked in tests/optimizations/pass/device_transfer_plugin.vx,
// since a test in this directory has to fail.
//
// RUN: not vxc %s --machine fleet/xeon-e5-2666v3.vx 2>&1 | FileCheck %s
// CHECK: E6002
// CHECK-SAME: Cannot transfer from CPU_DRAM to SMEM
// CHECK-SAME: no hardware path exists

fn main() -> i32 {
  let mut tile = Tensor<f32, [64, 64]>::uninit();
  let tile_s = transfer(tile, Memory::SMEM);
  return 0;
}
